Reranker-lane contention control, for the Hindsight HA add-on.

Applied in the Dockerfile with `patch -p1 -d /app/api` against the API package
COPY-ed from the digest-pinned upstream image (v0.7.1 / 779e3140). When
bumping HINDSIGHT_IMAGE, regenerate this patch against the new source — the
build fails loudly here if the context drifts.

Problem (measured on the N150, 2026-07-20): the local cross-encoder rerank is
the serialized section of every recall — ~6.5s per 100-pair job with
RERANKER_LOCAL_MAX_CONCURRENT=1 — and the executor queue is FIFO. Any queue
depth >= 3 pushes the tail past the 15s recall handler deadline (504).
Consolidation batches issue one internal recall per fact through the same
lane (observed: recall=283.9s per batch), starving interactive recalls for
minutes (the 75-80% 504 window). Even without consolidation, a 6-deep
interactive burst 504s at 66% (measured: 7.4s, 13.8s, then 4x 504@15s), with
the CPU already saturated (~390% of 400%), so raising the reranker
concurrency would only thrash.

Three changes, all no-ops unless their env var is set:

1. engine/consolidation/consolidator.py _find_related_observations: set a
   contextvar marking the recall as consolidation-internal.

2. engine/memory_engine.py recall step 4: when the contextvar is set,
   HINDSIGHT_API_CONSOLIDATION_RECALL_RERANKER=rrf, and the configured
   reranker is the local provider (the contended lane), use an
   RRFPassthroughCrossEncoder-backed reranker instead of the local
   cross-encoder. Upstream's is_passthrough_reranker path in
   apply_combined_scoring seeds CE scores from RRF rank, so ordering
   degrades gracefully; consolidation is LLM-mediated and quality-tolerant
   (it already runs at budget=low).

3. engine/memory_engine.py + engine/cross_encoder.py busy-degrade: when
   HINDSIGHT_API_RERANKER_BUSY_PASSTHROUGH_THRESHOLD=N (>0) and the local
   reranker lane already has >= N unfinished jobs, an interactive recall
   falls back to RRF-passthrough ordering instead of queueing toward a
   certain 504 — degraded ranking beats an "unavailable" outcome for
   consumers. Occupancy is tracked in LocalSTCrossEncoder: incremented on
   submit in the event loop, released by a done-callback on the concurrent
   future, which fires exactly once for normal completion, worker exception,
   or cancellation of a still-queued job — and NOT before a started thread
   actually finishes, so doomed post-deadline work stays visible to the
   check and a cancelled queued job cannot leak the counter.
   Atomicity of check-then-submit: the reranker initializes eagerly at
   startup (lazy_reranker defaults false), after which there is no event-
   loop suspension between the step-4 pending_jobs() check and the submit
   increment; the API and worker share one event loop.
   The build runs build-smoke/rerank_occupancy_smoke.py as a regression
   gate for the accounting.

